sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch07-18> a delphi:Claim ;
    rdfs:label "Pushed or jiggled spheres do not reach densest packing" ;
    dct:description "Unlike identical coins in two dimensions, which readily form the densest hexagonal packing when pushed together, balls in three dimensions pushed together or jiggled almost always get stuck and essentially never reach the densest repetitive packing, which seems obtainable only by careful one-by-one placement." ;
    delphi:context "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 7, “Mechanisms in Programs and Nature”; section “The Problem of Satisfying Constraints” (from p. 342), p. 349. Scope: packing of identical spheres and circles under physical compression or agitation" ;
    dct:type "model-of-nature" .
}

